module Float32 exposing (fromFloat, toFloat)

{-| The conversions of a 32-bit IEEE 754 floating-point number.

The **type** is [Basics.Float32](Basics#Float32), for the reason
[Int64](Int64) gives: `+`, `/`, `isNaN` and the rest work on a `Float32` with
no import at all.

**When to use one.** For data that is single precision on the other side of an
interface -- a vertex buffer, an audio sample, a protobuf `float` -- and not to
compute in. `docs/arithmetic.md` A8 makes every operation round to single
precision, which on JavaScript is a `Math.fround` after every one of them, so a
`Float32` is slower than a `Float` and less accurate on purpose.

This module has no `minValue` and no `maxValue`, and the omission is
deliberate: a float has three different candidates for "smallest" -- the most
negative finite value, the smallest positive normal and the smallest positive
subnormal -- and naming one of them `minValue` would be a trap. `docs/m1b-ryu.md`
is where the family of float constants belongs, beside the digits.

@docs fromFloat, toFloat

-}

import Basics exposing (Float, Float32)


{-| Round a `Float` to single precision.

Total: a value too large for a `Float32` becomes an infinity, which is what
IEEE rounding does and is not the saturation A10 gives an integer conversion.

    Float32.toFloat (Float32.fromFloat 0.5) == 0.5

-}
@prim("f64_to_f32")
fromFloat : Float -> Float32


{-| Widen to a `Float`. Exact: every `Float32` is a `Float`.
-}
@prim("f32_to_f64")
toFloat : Float32 -> Float
